Based on the error log, the `retained_mem_api` test suite is failing on the `qemu_cortex_m3` board. The failure occurs in `test_clear` when verifying that data can be written and read back correctly after the retained memory has been cleared. This suggests an issue with memory access.

Looking at the devicetree overlay for `qemu_cortex_m3` (`tests/drivers/retained_mem/api/boards/qemu_cortex_m3.overlay`), a new memory region for retained memory is defined:

```dts
	sram@2000ffe0 {
		compatible = "zephyr,memory-region", "mmio-sram";
		reg = <0x20010000 0x20>;
		ranges = <0x0 0x2000ffe0 0x20>;
		...
	};
```

The `qemu_cortex_m3` board has 64KB of SRAM, from `0x20000000` to `0x2000FFFF`. The `reg` property of the `sram@2000ffe0` node is set to `<0x20010000 0x20>`, which is just outside the valid SRAM address range. The `retained-ram` driver uses the `reg` property of its parent node (`sram@2000ffe0`) to determine the memory address, so it attempts to access an out-of-bounds memory location. This leads to undefined behavior and the observed test failure.

The node name `sram@2000ffe0` and the `ranges` property `<0x0 0x2000ffe0 0x20>` both indicate that the intended address is `0x2000ffe0`. This address is within the valid SRAM range, in an area made available by shrinking the main `sram0` region.

The fix is to correct the `reg` property to use the intended address `0x2000ffe0`.

